        </div>
       </div>
       <div class="sc-egiyK kKHsVi pf-r pf-r-eh" draggable="true" data-pf-type="Row" style="--s-xs: 15px; --s-lg: 0px;">
        <div class="pf-c" style="--c-xs: 12; --c-md: 12; --c-lg: 12;">
         <div draggable="true" data-pf-type="Column" class="sc-bilyIR egurxi">
          <div class="sc-jrQzAO gACUHK pf-icon-left" data-scrolling="false" data-multiple="false" draggable="true" data-pf-type="Accordion">
           <div data-pf-type="Accordion.Content.Wrapper" class="sc-kDTinF cmxFnd">
            <button type="button" class="sc-furwcr bQmZNl pf-anchor" data-active="false" data-pf-type="Accordion.Header"><i class="pfa pfa-plus pfa-arrow"></i><span data-text-editing="true" class="">What’s the best way to store coffee from your store?</span></button>
            <div data-pf-expandable="true" data-pf-type="Accordion.Content" class="sc-pVTFL hKxRej" style="display: none;">
             <div class="sc-egiyK kKHsVi pf-r pf-r-eh" draggable="true" data-pf-type="Row" style="--s-xs: 15px; --s-lg: 0px;">
              <div class="pf-c" style="--c-xs: 12; --c-md: 12; --c-lg: 12;">
               <div draggable="true" data-pf-type="Column" class="sc-bilyIR egurxi">
                <p class="sc-jQrDum eEXLPW pf-text-1 pf-caret-color-transparent pf-word-wrap-break-word" contenteditable="true" spellcheck="false" draggable="true" data-pf-type="Paragraph3">Once roasted, coffee begins to lose its flavor the longer it’s exposed to air and moisture. We recommend that you store it in an airtight container in a cool, dark place and grind it just before brewing. Coffee in an unopened FlavorLock bag will stay fresh for several months.</p>
               </div>
              </div>
             </div>
            </div>
           </div>
           <div data-pf-type="Accordion.Content.Wrapper" class="sc-kDTinF cmxFnd">
            <button type="button" class="sc-furwcr bQmZNl pf-anchor" data-active="false" data-pf-type="Accordion.Header"><i class="pfa pfa-plus pfa-arrow"></i><span data-text-editing="true" class="">What kind of grind should I use for making coffee?</span></button>
            <div data-pf-expandable="true" data-pf-type="Accordion.Content" class="sc-pVTFL hKxRej" style="display: none;">
             <div class="sc-egiyK kKHsVi pf-r pf-r-eh" draggable="true" data-pf-type="Row" style="--s-xs: 15px; --s-lg: 0px;">
              <div class="pf-c" style="--c-xs: 12; --c-md: 12; --c-lg: 12;">
               <div draggable="true" data-pf-type="Column" class="sc-bilyIR egurxi">
                <p class="sc-jQrDum eEXLPW pf-text-1 pf-caret-color-transparent pf-word-wrap-break-word" contenteditable="true" spellcheck="false" draggable="true" data-pf-type="Paragraph3">Drip makers perform best with a grind specifically for a flat bottom or cone type filter. Espresso machines are grind-sensitive and require a very fine grind, 30-35 seconds in a blade grinder. Slow and fast shots are often caused by a grind that is too fine or too coarse, or tamping coffee too hard or not firmly enough.</p>
               </div>
              </div>
             </div>
            </div>
           </div>
           <div data-pf-type="Accordion.Content.Wrapper" class="sc-kDTinF cmxFnd">
            <button type="button" class="sc-furwcr bQmZNl pf-anchor" data-active="true" data-pf-type="Accordion.Header"><i class="pfa pfa-minus pfa-arrow"></i><span data-text-editing="true" class="">What are your organic coffee standards?</span></button>
            <div data-pf-expandable="true" data-pf-type="Accordion.Content" class="sc-pVTFL hKxRej" style="display: block;">
             <div class="sc-egiyK kKHsVi pf-r pf-r-eh" draggable="true" data-pf-type="Row" style="--s-xs: 15px; --s-lg: 0px;">
              <div class="pf-c" style="--c-xs: 12; --c-md: 12; --c-lg: 12;">
               <div draggable="true" data-pf-type="Column" class="sc-bilyIR egurxi">
                <p class="sc-jQrDum eEXLPW pf-text-1 pf-caret-color-transparent pf-word-wrap-break-word" contenteditable="true" spellcheck="false" draggable="true" data-pf-type="Paragraph3">All of our 100% Organic coffees are guaranteed organic by the United States Department of Agriculture (USDA) and are grown in natural, eco-friendly conditions in Central and South America. Organic certification verifies that farms, crop harvesters, and handling operations comply with organic regulations to maintain their organic integrity.</p>
               </div>
              </div>
             </div>
            </div>
           </div>
           <div data-pf-type="Accordion.Content.Wrapper" class="sc-kDTinF cmxFnd">
            <button type="button" class="sc-furwcr bQmZNl pf-anchor" data-active="false" data-pf-type="Accordion.Header"><i class="pfa pfa-plus pfa-arrow"></i><span data-text-editing="true" class="">Which coffee-based drink has the most caffeine?</span></button>
            <div data-pf-expandable="true" data-pf-type="Accordion.Content" class="sc-pVTFL hKxRej" style="display: none;">
             <div class="sc-egiyK kKHsVi pf-r pf-r-eh" draggable="true" data-pf-type="Row" style="--s-xs: 15px; --s-lg: 0px;">
              <div class="pf-c" style="--c-xs: 12; --c-md: 12; --c-lg: 12;">
               <div draggable="true" data-pf-type="Column" class="sc-bilyIR egurxi">
                <p class="sc-jQrDum eEXLPW pf-text-1 pf-caret-color-transparent pf-word-wrap-break-word" contenteditable="true" spellcheck="false" draggable="true" data-pf-type="Paragraph3">Most people think Espresso Roast has the most caffeine, but brewed coffee is proven to have more. A single 2 oz. Double Espresso shot has about 80 milligrams of caffeine. Whereas an average 12 oz. brewed coffee has about 120 milligrams of caffeine. And our Blast Off Strong Roast formula provides over 1.5x more caffeine than other coffee.</p>
               </div>
              </div>
             </div>
            </div>
           </div>
           <div data-pf-type="Accordion.Content.Wrapper" class="sc-kDTinF cmxFnd">
            <button type="button" class="sc-furwcr bQmZNl pf-anchor" data-active="false" data-pf-type="Accordion.Header"><i class="pfa pfa-plus pfa-arrow"></i><span data-text-editing="true" class="">What is the difference between Arabica and Robusta beans?</span></button>
            <div data-pf-expandable="true" data-pf-type="Accordion.Content" class="sc-pVTFL hKxRej" style="display: none;">
             <div class="sc-egiyK kKHsVi pf-r pf-r-eh" draggable="true" data-pf-type="Row" style="--s-xs: 15px; --s-lg: 0px;">
              <div class="pf-c" style="--c-xs: 12; --c-md: 12; --c-lg: 12;">
               <div draggable="true" data-pf-type="Column" class="sc-bilyIR egurxi">
                <p class="sc-jQrDum eEXLPW pf-text-1 pf-caret-color-transparent pf-word-wrap-break-word" contenteditable="true" spellcheck="false" draggable="true" data-pf-type="Paragraph3">Arabica Beans have a wide range in taste and smell. They are a more delicate bean produced in the tropical regions at a high elevation. They are more expensive due to the hands-on production process. Robusta Beans have a neutral to harsh taste and an oatmeal-like texture. They grow in low elevations, are heartier for rough handling, and yield higher pounds per acre.</p>
               </div>
              </div>
             </div>
            </div>
           </div>
